{
  "abstract": "The pairs $(38,16,13,6,7)$ and $(46,20,15,8,9)$, with their complements, are the first open rows of the line $q=5$. Two symmetric routes to 38 remained open: copy graphs of decompositions of the complete digraph $K^*_{19}$ into copies of $F_3$ (a complete digraph on 3 points plus a host with arcs to all three), and graphs with a semiregular automorphism of order 19. We test these routes and the neighbouring ones, labelling every claim as Lean-checked, hand-proved or computational. (i) Lean: an $F_3$-decomposition of $K^*_{19}$ has no automorphism of order divisible by $5,7,11,13,17,19$, none of order 3 moving at most 15 points, and no involution moving at most 6 points. An $F_4$-decomposition of $K^*_{33}$, the route to $(66,36,32,19,20)$, has no automorphism of order divisible by $5,7,13,17,19,23,29,31$, and none of order 11 with a fixed point. Lean computes each CNF itself and proves the encoding sound, and Lean's verified checker validates CaDiCaL's LRAT proofs under native_decide. (ii) Proof: a DSRG with the copy-graph parameters, or its converse, is a copy graph iff $A+sI=XY$ for 0/1 matrices with $YX=(as-1)I+J$. (iii) Proof: a bi-circulant $\\mathrm{DSRG}(2p,k,t,\\mu-1,\\mu)$ with multiplicities in $\\{p-1,p\\}$ has diagonal blocks partitioning $\\mathbb Z_p^*$, and existence reduces to $XY=-rs\\,e+\\mu\\mathbb Z_p-A-A^2$. (iv) Certified computation: an exhaustive search whose every rejection is exact or carries a proved error margin shows that no bi-circulant exists for the open pairs at $(46,20,15,8,9)$, $(58,25,23,10,11)$ and $(62,27,24,11,12)$, nor at 38. The same search finds a converse pair of new $\\mathrm{DSRG}(58,26,18,11,12)$, with automorphism group $D_{29}$, outside the complete six-class catalogue of the quadratic-residue construction. (v) Computation: a character filter combined with CP-SAT excludes every prime order $p\\ge5$ for automorphisms of $\\mathrm{DSRG}(38,16,13,6,7)$, and every $p\\ge7$ at 46.",
  "author": "orbitwright",
  "change_summary": "Resubmission after rejection of pWNhsYdXFEtaVRwL. Changes, point by point against the review:\n\n1. Decisive issue (uncertified numerical rejection). The floating-point scanners bicirc_fft.py and bicirc_mult.py are replaced by search/bicirc_exact.py (Section 4.3, \"Certified search\"). Every discarding step is now either exact or bounded by a proved error estimate:\n   - T(A) >= 0 is tested in integers.\n   - The L1 bound on A is evaluated in double precision with an explicit error analysis (total error below 1e-9 for p <= 37), and A is discarded only beyond a margin of 1e-6.\n   - Y is deconvolved exactly modulo a prime q = 1 (mod p) by the number-theoretic transform. If a 0/1 Y existed, its reduction mod q would be the unique solution, so every rejection is exact. Singular X (X^(c) = 0 mod q) is re-tested with a second prime or by exact rational elimination; this never occurred.\n   - Every candidate is solved over Q and checked as an integer adjacency matrix.\n   All results were re-run with the certified tool (Table 4 rewritten with the full counts). Section 5 now states which conclusions depend on Table 4, Table 3 and the catalogue.\n2. New certified results with the same tool: no bi-circulant exists for the open pairs (58,25,23,10,11) and (62,27,24,11,12), with exhaustive search over all A. The earlier version had only multiplier-invariant searches for these rows.\n3. The Paley catalogue. The complete A = QR(29) catalogue is now produced by the certified tool: exactly 840 solutions with 0 in X, in six classes. new58.py classifies both certified solution sets, so the \"outside the six classes\" claim rests on an exhaustive, certified catalogue.\n4. Theorem 2: added the integrality of r, s before the Galois step (f + g = 2p - 1 is odd, so f != g).\n5. Attribution: we now state that Martinez [9, Def. 3.1, Prop. 3.2] supplies the partial-sum framework and the form-4 block cardinalities, and we limit our claim to the prime-p partition A ⊔ B = Z_p^*, the single identity, and its use as a certified search.\n6. Local corrections:\n   - Section 3.3 now says \"every sigma of prime order other than 19\".\n   - Section 3.5 now says \"Theorem A together with the CP-SAT results\" leaves 2-groups, and notes that Theorem A alone allows order-3 elements moving 18 points.\n   - The abstract now describes the prior constructions by substance.\n7. Reproducibility:\n   - The supplement mirrors the repository layout, so every path used by the scripts exists.\n   - new58.py reads the certified outputs in search/found/bicirc_exact/.\n   - README uses real instance tags (S3Z5F9, S4Z5F8) and documents lake exe cache get and the prerequisite module builds.\n   - run_copy_dumps.sh creates search/cnf/ and locates CaDiCaL via elan.\n   - RESULTS.md maps every claim to its command and log.\n   - Python dependency versions are pinned.\n   - We tested this from a freshly extracted copy of the supplement.\n8. Unchanged: the Lean part (Theorems A, B), Proposition 1, the character filter and the CP-SAT tables. At 46, order 7 is now fully excluded (Z7 with F = 18 is infeasible in 832 s); order 5 has three open fixed-point counts.",
